  "text": "To factor the given expression \\(x^4 + 6x^3 - x^2 - 24x + 16 + x^3 + 2x^2 - 6x - 8\\), we first combine like terms.\n\nCombine the \\(x^3\\) terms:\n\\[6x^3 + x^3 = 7x^3\\]\n\nCombine the \\(x^2\\) terms:\n\\[-x^2 + 2x^2 = x^2\\]\n\nCombine the \\(x\\) terms:\n\\[-24x - 6x = -30x\\]\n\nCombine the constant terms:\n\\[16 - 8 = 8\\]\n\nSo, the expression simplifies to:\n\\[x^4 + 7x^3 + x^2 - 30x + 8\\]\n\nNext, we look for possible rational roots using the Rational Root Theorem. The potential rational roots are the factors of the constant term (8) divided by the factors of the leading coefficient (1). The factors of 8 are \\(\\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 4, \\pm 8\\). We test these values to see if they are roots.\n\nTest \\(x = 1\\):\n\\[1^4 + 7(1)^3 + 1^2 - 30(1) + 8 = 1 + 7 + 1 - 30 + 8 = -11 \\neq 0\\]\n\nTest \\(x = -1\\):\n\\[(-1)^4 + 7(-1)^3 + (-1)^2 - 30(-1) + 8 = 1 - 7 + 1 + 30 + 8 = 33 \\neq 0\\]\n\nTest \\(x = 2\\):\n\\[2^4 + 7(2)^3 + 2^2 - 30(2) + 8 = 16 + 56 + 4 - 60 + 8 = 34 \\neq 0\\]\n\nTest \\(x = -2\\):\n\\[(-2)^4 + 7(-2)^3 + (-2)^2 - 30(-2) + 8 = 16 - 56 + 4 + 60 + 8 = 32 \\neq 0\\]\n\nTest \\(x = 4\\):\n\\[4^4 + 7(4)^3 + 4^2 - 30(4) + 8 = 256 + 448 + 16 - 120 + 8 = 600 \\neq 0\\]\n\nTest \\(x = -4\\):\n\\[(-4)^4 + 7(-4)^3 + (-4)^2 - 30(-4) + 8 = 256 - 448 + 16 + 120 + 8 = 0\\]\n\nSince \\(x = -4\\) is a root, we can factor out \\((x + 4)\\) from the polynomial. We perform polynomial division or synthetic division to find the quotient:\n\nUsing synthetic division with root \\(-4\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r|rrrrr}\n-4 & 1 & 7 & 1 & -30 & 8 \\\\\n   &   & -4 & -12 & 44 & -48 \\\\\n\\hline\n   & 1 & 3 & -11 & 14 & 0 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThe quotient is \\(x^3 + 3x^2 - 11x + 2\\). Now, we need to factor \\(x^3 + 3x^2 - 11x + 2\\). We test the potential rational roots again. The factors of 2 are \\(\\pm 1, \\pm 2\\).\n\nTest \\(x = 1\\):\n\\[1^3 + 3(1)^2 - 11(1) + 2 = 1 + 3 - 11 + 2 = -5 \\neq 0\\]\n\nTest \\(x = -1\\):\n\\[(-1)^3 + 3(-1)^2 - 11(-1) + 2 = -1 + 3 + 11 + 2 = 15 \\neq 0\\]\n\nTest \\(x = 2\\):\n\\[2^3 + 3(2)^2 - 11(2) + 2 = 8 + 12 - 22 + 2 = 0\\]\n\nSince \\(x = 2\\) is a root, we can factor out \\((x - 2)\\) from the polynomial. We perform polynomial division or synthetic division to find the quotient:\n\nUsing synthetic division with root \\(2\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r|rrrr}\n2 & 1 & 3 & -11 & 2 \\\\\n  &   & 2 & 10 & -2 \\\\\n\\hline\n  & 1 & 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThe quotient is \\(x^2 + 5x - 1\\). Therefore, the polynomial \\(x^3 + 3x^2 - 11x + 2\\) can be factored as \\((x - 2)(x^2 + 5x - 1)\\).\n\nPutting it all together, the original expression factors as:\n\\[x^4 + 7x^3 + x^2 - 30x + 8 = (x + 4)(x - 2)(x^2 + 5x - 1)\\]\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(x + 4)(x - 2)(x^2 + 5x - 1)}\n\\]",
